    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "A current limitation is that it is not possible to really use multigrid clients to their full potential while also using\nthe TUI. this is a bit inconvenient as the TUI is meant to be  just another GUI, not something which limits other GUI:s. another limitation is that the legacy single grid design only will allow the same view to be shared across clients, while multigrid mode is intended to be extended eventually to support multiple views (there are some concrete sketches like ext_windows and ext_tabline to bring back to life for this)\n\nI think the way forward is to rework the TUI to use multigrid events. In https://github.com/bfredl/nvim-ui-client I have sketched out a multigrid based TUI implementation, although it is not usable as a daily driver just yet.\n\nDone:\n\n- [x] reusable RPC/UIState abstraction (for GUI:s)\n- [x] xterm compat support\n- [x] basic multigrid prototype\n- [x] 'mouse' support\n- [x] cmdline/messages handled via ui2 (omg, they are just windows!)\n\nTo be done:\n\n- [ ] check tmux/screen support + more\n- [ ] a lot of redraw glitches left\n- [ ] Redesigned and efficient multigrid support\n- [ ] \"ui1\" fallback (handle msg_grid_pos) if we still support old school messages for a few more neovim cycles\n\nAlso I plan to do some server-side long time due improvements in parallel. Like I think what makes sense (for the current \"iteration\" with nvim side layout control) is to move statuslines to their own grids. Then there will be no `grid_line` events left for the \"default grid\" and UI:s no longer will need to keep a separate state buffer for that (saves a lot of over allocated space).",
